# Plaintext template for deploy/mcp-secrets.sops.env.
#
# To use:
#   1. cp deploy/mcp-secrets.sops.env.example /tmp/mcp.env
#   2. fill in real values (umask 077 first!)
#   3. sops --encrypt /tmp/mcp.env > deploy/mcp-secrets.sops.env
#   4. shred -u /tmp/mcp.env
#   5. git add deploy/mcp-secrets.sops.env
#
# See docs/mcp.md and docs/secrets.md.
#
# This template file is shipped as plaintext so operators can see
# the expected variable names without decrypting anything. Real
# .sops.env files are encrypted at rest -- never commit a plaintext
# version under the canonical name.
#
# NAMES MATTER (corrected 2026-07-25). The gateway resolves a secret by
# the name the UPSTREAM catalog declares for that server, not by an
# environment-variable name of our choosing. This file previously listed
# GITHUB_TOKEN / FIRECRAWL_API_KEY / HF_TOKEN / CONTEXT7_API_KEY; none of
# those would ever have resolved, so the secret-bearing servers would
# have failed silently -- the same way the rest of the MCP config did.
#
# Two former entries are gone because they are not needed: upstream
# converted hugging-face and context7 to `type: remote` endpoints that
# connect anonymously. Verified live -- 4 tools and 2 tools respectively,
# with no credentials supplied. Add a context7 key only if you hit its
# anonymous rate limit; it is optional upstream.
#
# UNVERIFIED: no tool has been invoked with a real token. Both servers
# below enumerate their tools without credentials, so a wrong value here
# will not surface until you actually call one.

github.personal_access_token=ghp_replace_with_real_token
firecrawl.api_key=fc-replace_with_real_key
